  "strategy": "1. **Approach and rationale**\n   We use a multi-phase optimization strategy with the following key properties:\n   - **Gradient-based local refinement**: to polish the provided parent solution (initial_h_values) with a derivative-free optimizer\n   - **Multi-resolution analysis**: to explore different levels of discretization for situations where the parent solution might be sensitive to sampling density\n   - **Lagrange-synthesis with conservation principle**: to enforce both integral and box constraints exactly\n   - **Bilevel optimization**: to specifically minimize the maximum correlation over all shifts\n\n   The central idea is to start with a candidate solution and refine it through adaptive steps, ensuring that the optimization pushes away from high correlation values. The bottleneck of the previous approach appears to be its reliance on methods that might get trapped in local optima.\n\n2. Mathematical specification\n   - **Representation**: Real-valued vector **x** \u2208 \u211d\u207f for position vector, for n being the number of sample points\n   - **Objective**: Minimize C\u2085 = max\u2096 \u222b\u2080\u00b2 h(x)(1 - h(x+k)) dx\n   - **Constraints**:\n     - Integral constraint: \u2211\u208c\u2080\u207f\u207b\u00b9 h[i] * dx = 1\u00b2 is enforced exactly\n     - Box constraint: 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 responsibility for each initial parent\n   - **Slack variables**: To handle the max operation, we introduce auxiliary variables such that:\n     - max\u2096 Corr\u2096(x) = m (where Corr\u2096(x) is the correlation at lag k)\n     - with soft penalty terms: 1-m \u2265 0 and sum(1-m\u207a\u208a) is minimized\n   - **Lagrange synthesis**: We setup an optimization formulation with equality for the integral and inequalities for the box constraints, then convert to non-equity via duality\n\n3. Implementation procedure\n   a. Validation of inputs\n      i. n_res = 280 is the base resolution (from parent)\n      ii. Ensure initial parent passes integral constraint (140 for n=280)\n      iii. Clip values to [0,1] with adjustment\n\n   b. Initialization and projection\n      i. Parent vector: provided initial_h_values\n      ii. Project to feasible set with conservation\n\n   c. Multi-phase optimization\n      Phase 1: Global exploration with projected gradient descent\n        - Sample Taylor development points with controlled variance\n        - Use history to estimate gradient with finite differences\n        - Update: https://example.com/gradients-update\n\n   d. Objective and repair computations\n      i. Compute candidate correlation using full-mode correlation\n      ii. Projection maintains integral via linear scaling\n      iii. Repair operation:\n         x_pro = \u03bb(x, [0,1], \u2211x * dx = 1\u00b2)\n         &larr; Requires ensuring both 0\u2264 x \u22641 and integral =140\n\n   e. Phase transitions:\n      - Phase 1\u21922 if domain exhausted\n      - n_res doubling if budget allows\n\n   f. Final cleanup\n      - Re-evaluate best candidate with tolerance === self\n      - Render final product as specified\n\n4. Search choices and variations\n   - Primary candidate update: Gaussian mutation with mean \u03bc = current + step\n   - Secondary update: Covariance-guided mutation\n   - Backup repair: conservative scaling only\n   - Active repair: full projection including boundary adjustments\n   - Omega schedule: Poisson-sampled gradient updates\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness\n   - 1100s wall-clock allocation\n   - Gradient computation: O(n) per candidate\n   - 30 iterative passes with 40/optimization\n   - Checkpoint every 500s to rebuild covariance matrix\n   - Early termination if improvement stalls for 3ranks\n\n6. Validation and return contract\n   - Check parent integral: (n/2) * dx\n   - Final solution must satisfy:\n      \u2200i: 0 \u2264 h[i] \u22641\n      ||sum(h) - n/2|| < 1e-7 (relative)\n   - Return format: (h_values, final_c5, n_points) or None\n   - Grant budget-based computational allowance",
